{
  "requirement": "In Markdown, escaped roles and role-looking text in inline code, fenced code, or raw HTML MUST remain literal and MUST NOT count as references. Raw HTML means an HTML block, an HTML comment, or an inline HTML tag including its attributes; text between inline tags is ordinary Markdown. A `{req}` directive inside a fenced code example and a legacy inline `{req}` role MUST NOT define a requirement.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the implementation covers every obligation, it is marked completely, and the tests cover the interesting cases. The tests were not run. I judged them by reading their code.\n\n**Implementation, each obligation:**\n- **Code and HTML are protected.** `protected_ranges` (src/lib.rs:738) parses the Markdown with pulldown-cmark and collects four kinds of range: `Start(CodeBlock)`, `Code`, `Html` and `InlineHtml`.\n  - Fenced code is covered by `Start(CodeBlock)`.\n  - Inline code is covered by `Code`.\n  - HTML blocks and block-level comments are covered by `Html`.\n  - Inline tags, including their attributes, and inline comments are covered by `InlineHtml`.\n  - Text between inline tags is not an HTML event, so it stays ordinary Markdown, exactly as the requirement says.\n- **Roles are skipped when protected or escaped.** `reference_roles` (src/lib.rs:678) skips any `{` that `is_escaped` (an odd number of preceding backslashes) or that falls inside a protected range (`is_protected`). In Markdown mode it also protects the first line of a term definition body, which is declared by other requirements. In non-Markdown mode (Rust comments, plain text) it applies no protection.\n- **Directives and legacy roles do not define requirements.** `definition_blocks` (src/lib.rs:516) recognises only line-start colon-fence `{req}`/`{example}`/`{definition}` openers. It skips any opener or closer line that overlaps a code-block range (`overlaps_protected`). The legacy inline `{req}` role is never parsed as a definition, and `reference_roles` does not recognise it.\n- **Every call site goes through this logic.** The callers I found are evidence reference collection (`.md` files use Markdown mode), the book preprocessor, example and anchor checks, and term dependency scanning. None of them has a separate scanner that could bypass it. `index::marker_ranges` also uses `protected_ranges` and `overlaps_protected`, but only for the `{requirements-index}` marker, which is not a role.\n- **Marking is complete.** All of `definition_blocks`, `overlaps_protected`, `reference_roles`, `protected_ranges`, `is_protected` and `is_escaped` carry `{impl}` for this requirement. I found no unmarked code that implements part of it.\n\n**Tests (all marked `{test}`):**\n- `code_examples_and_legacy_roles_remain_literal` (src/lib.rs:992) covers an inline-code `{req}`, a `:::{req}` inside a fenced block, and a legacy `{req}` role. It also checks that a real directive still renders.\n- `markdown_examples_and_escaped_references_stay_literal` (src/lib.rs:1187) covers inline code, a fenced block and a backslash-escaped role through the full book preprocessor. No badge is produced.\n- `roles_in_raw_html_stay_literal` (src/lib.rs:1212) covers an HTML block, a block comment, an inline comment and an attribute value, all literal. The role between `<em>` tags counts as exactly one reference. In non-Markdown mode all five occurrences are counted.\n- `colon_directives_render_markdown_bodies_and_legacy_roles_stay_literal` (src/lib.rs:1228) covers an inline legacy `{req}` role, which defines nothing.\n- `references_in_requirement_bodies_are_scanned_as_markdown` (src/lib.rs:1243) checks that a fenced example inside a requirement body is not counted as a reference.\n- `source_references_are_covered_by_evidence_inputs` (src/evidence.rs:1638) checks that the CI reference collection ignores a role in inline code and in a fenced block of a `.md` file. If they were counted, `missing.example` would produce errors.\n\n**Minor gaps:** there are no explicit tests for a double-backslash (which is not an escape) or for an indented code block. Both follow directly from the straightforward implementation, so I do not count them as gaps.",
  "inputs": [
